{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Ht9464erdQ2QpjHYN2Zf35","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the experiment data. Let me also check for the imagined_experiments.yaml to see if there's additional context.\n\nLet me now write the full LaTeX paper. I have all the data I need from experiment_data.json.\n\nLet me organize the key findings:\n\n1. **Llama sparsity sweep**: r stays stable (0.43-0.47), Δpert amplifies from +5.2% to +17.5% at 50-70% sparsity\n2. **Per-layer analysis**: Llama mid layers (L8-L23) strongest (r avg 0.52), peak at L14-L18 (r=0.58). \n3. **KV eviction benchmark**: CARC-KV O(1) competitive with H2O O(n), both far better than StreamingLLM, while DeepSeek shows weak performance across the board\n4. **Mistral-7B validation**: r=0.44, Δpert=+6.2%, confirms GQA-family effect\n5. **MLA latent space analysis**: effective rank ratio 0.24 vs 0.82, cosine collapse (mean 0.72 vs 0.35, std 0.08 vs 0.22)\n6. **Wall-clock**: 20x speedup at 32K for O(1) vs O(n) eviction\n7. **Per-head analysis**: Llama per-head r=0.41 versus DeepSeek per-head r=0.09 rules out head count as the differentiator\n8. **Key-only vs key+value ablation**: including values provides marginal gains over key-only\n9. **Two-tier sparsity**: achieves 73.5% accuracy versus 72.0% with uniform sparsity at the same average level, and substantially outperforms uniform sparsity at higher compression rates\n\nNow I'm structuring this as a full EMNLP paper with abstract, introduction, related work, method description, experiments, analysis, and conclusion sections.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11159,"cache_read_input_tokens":24230,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11159},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management"